hi  welcome to another episode of cold  fusion where i cover anything in science  technology business or history  elizabeth holmes was the darling of  silicon valley a brilliant thinker a  fearless empowerment icon she envisioned  a revolutionary product called the  edison that could fit on a desk and yet  perform over a thousand medical tests  and diagnoses with a single drop of  blood  no longer was testing slow and  cumbersome with big machines it was the  iphone of the medical field  despite having no medical degree and  dropping out of university elizabeth was  featured on the cover of forbes and  fortune magazine  everyone from the walton family to  rupert murdoch was jumping on board to  invest her company theranos was going to  revolutionize medicine the theronos  board included the former ceo of wells  fargo and the former u.s secretary of  state henry kissinger at its peak  theranos managed to master a company  valuation of 10 billion  but it wasn't to last  in 2015 the wall street journal  published a series of articles that  suggested that its devices were flawed  and inaccurate and from there skepticism  began to grow the theranos story was one  of the biggest scandals of the 2010s and  it was one of the most viewed episodes  on this channel so if you need to brush  up on the entire story be sure to watch  the original episode first  in 2018 holmes was indicted by the  department of justice federal  prosecutors accused the 37 year old of  defrauding investors doctors and  patients  holmes pled not guilty to nine wire  fraud counts and two conspiracy camps  there's a possible prison sentence of up  to 20 years for each conviction  she also faces a fine of 250 000 plus  restitution for each count her trial  started in august of 2021 and earlier in  the week she was found guilty on four  counts  in this episode we'll take a look at how  it all went down  [Music]  you are watching  tv  after the 2018 indictment elizabeth  holmes mostly went under the radar  but in 2019 she was spotted in san  francisco with her new partner luxury  hotel heir billy evans  elizabeth holmes might be facing up to  20 years in prison but she sure looks  happy prancing around san francisco  that's the disgraced tycoon and her  fiance billy evans holmes has been  arrested and charged with massive fraud  elizabeth a lot of young women looked up  to you especially in tech what would you  have to say to those young women  she wouldn't be running free for long  though  when the trial did begin in 2021 the  main crux of the case was as follows did  elizabeth holmes knowingly deceive or  was she just a struggling entrepreneur  desperately buying some time to fix a  product in good faith  the question here was intent  as james melendrez a former federal  prosecutor told wired magazine quote the  battleground is holmes's mental state  whether or not she had the intent to  commit fraud you have 12 jurors 12  people off the street who sit in a room  and decide what was in holmes's mind  witnesses included former employees who  had stated that holmes knowingly covered  up the fact that the machines that she  was touting didn't work the courts found  that holmes convinced investors to pile  in money by faking demos exaggerating  claims and printing pharmaceutical  company logos on theranos reports to  make them appear legitimate she even  made a claim of having a contract of the  us military to boost the company's image  during the proceedings all was laid bare  emails text messages internal documents  you name it  it all pointed to what we suspected  theranos was an absolute catastrophe  the government's first witness at the  holmes fraud trial was theranos  financial controller denise yam  when yam testified against theranos it  painted a bleak picture  the company had net losses of 16.2  million in 2010  27.2 million in 2011 57 million in 2012  and 92 million in 2013 the company made  no revenue in 2012 and 2013 and burned  through two million dollars per week in  2013. yam testified that theranos went  for years without having its financial  statements audited  by 2015 theranos had accumulated losses  of 585 million but a document was given  to investors that forecasted a revenue  for 2014 of 140 million and a revenue  for 2015 of 990 million former fisa  scientist shane webber took the stand he  testified that elizabeth holmes used the  pfizer logo in investor materials  without the company's permission the  purpose was to make theranos appear more  credible particularly in a study aimed  at validating their blood testing device  prosecutors stated that it was part of a  sham to convince walgreens to invest and  it worked walgreens invested 140 million  dollars in the blood testing company  holmes had placed the fisa logo at the  top of each page of a cancer study  report before sending it to walgreens  executives  the court was also shown an investor  booklet one page said quote theranos has  been comprehensively validated over the  course of the last seven years by 10 of  the 15 largest pharmaceutical companies  with hundreds of thousands of tests  processed  another witness former theronus employee  erica chuang took the stand  elizabeth claimed that the therinos  devices could do over a thousand tests  but according to erica in reality they  could do just 12 and not very well  she spoke about the large rate of  inaccuracies in theranos devices saying  that they regularly failed quality  control analysis executives would then  encourage scientists to cherry-pick and  manipulate data to make the results seem  more accurate than they were  because the therianos machines worked so  poorly most of the tests were done on  third-party machines  a nurse practitioner from arizona  testified that theranos machines were  giving wildly inconsistent blood results  there was one case where she had a  pregnant patient in her practice the  theronos machines gave mystifying  results which indicated that the  patient's pregnancy was going to fail  the patient who also testified  eventually successfully delivered a baby  the experience left nurse zackerman  quote very uncertain as to the validity  of the results  she also stated that she felt very  uncomfortable continuing to use the  services  it's important to note that medical  decisions can be based off the results  of blood tests so it's not a stretch to  say that holmes was potentially  endangering patients lives with her  faulty theronos devices  elizabeth holmes took the stand for  seven days  over this period she deflected blame  repeatedly she blamed her ex-boyfriend  and former business partner sunny  balwani for falsifying financial reports  she went on to say that he abused her  causing her mental anguish affecting her  performance and judgment at the company  elizabeth also blamed theranoss's  scientific advisors and therons's board  for the company's failings  prosecutors also focused on sunny and  elizabeth's luxurious lifestyle they  went on to describe how elizabeth holmes  traveled on private jets stayed in  luxury hotels and also of her reliance  on multiple assistants who took care of  home decorating clothes shopping and  jewelry  elizabeth holmes once celebrated as the  youngest self-made female billionaire in  the world left court a convicted felon  clutching the hands of her parents and  husband late monday a jury found her  guilty of four fraud related counts  after seven days of deliberations a jury  in san jose california found elizabeth  holmes guilty on four counts of why  fraud and conspiracy to commit white  fraud wire fraud in this case just means  fraud where the method of money transfer  from investors to theranos was a wire  transfer  the jury returned a verdict of not  guilty on another four counts and they  couldn't reach a unanimous verdict on  three  as mentioned holmes could be facing up  to 20 years for each conviction and a  combined one million dollar fine for the  four counts  so these charges mostly relate to the  investors and many say that it doesn't  account for the wrong doing against  patients who received bogus blood  results or other partners such as  walgreens who took faulty theranos  machines into their stores  but the thing that many people wonder is  how could she have gotten away with this  for so long in the first place many  further questions need to be asked  theranos was a classic story of hype  over substance  the reckless behaviour of sunny and  elizabeth holmes destroyed many  opportunities for legitimate research in  the field of multi-test blood work  now other companies working on this  problem will forever be looked at with  skepticism  speaking of which what were the issues  with the therianos multi-test medical  technology  well there are three main ones  number one getting reliable results from  a single drop of blood  number two performing multiple tests  with a single drop of blood and number  three shrinking the machine down to a  desktop size  so for the first one the idea of  performing a battery of tests from a  single drop of blood isn't new though  there are multiple medical studies that  state that the results of a single drop  of blood is intrinsically inconsistent  for example concentrations of things  such as white blood cell count and  hemoglobin can vary from drop to drop  when obtained from a finger  for the second issue running multiple  tests from a single drop of blood  appears to be impossible with current  technology right now to receive results  either compounds have to be added to the  blood or they have to be spun in a  centrifuge and sometimes other invasive  methods are used  so once these things are done it  destroys the ability for repeat testing  lastly shrinking the machine down to a  desktop size may have been possible but  only for a few select tests not the  thousands of tests that were claimed and  this is what most companies that dare to  remain in the field are working on today  and with that that's just about where  the theronoff story ends sunny balwani's  trial will be in the future  but for now the courts have decided that  elizabeth holmes was indeed trying to  scam investors out of billions and now  she could be facing decades in jail  so what do you guys think about the  verdict do you think she deserved more  or do you think justice was served  feel free to discuss in the comment  section below or in the cold fusion  discord  anyway that's about it from me hope you  all had a great christmas and new years  and i do hope to bring a lot of good  stuff this year on this channel this has  been to go though and you've been  watching cold fusion  thanks for watching and i'll see you  again soon for the next episode cheers  guys  have a good one  [Music]  [Applause]  cold fusion  it's me thinking

